---
/**
 * The front door.
 *
 * THE HIERARCHY IS THE FUNNEL (product/CUSTOMERS.md). A frightened trader on a phone asks "how
 * vulnerable am I", not "how vulnerable is the market", so the personal check leads and the
 * market aggregate is what they read once calm. Coverage, provenance and the record stay on the
 * page -- they are why the number is believable -- but below the fold.
 *
 * The form is a plain GET to check.html: no JavaScript on the entry point, one page owns the
 * lookup, and the resulting URL is shareable, which is the only way this spreads.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AddressHelp from '../components/AddressHelp.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import Prov from '../components/Prov.astro';
import { age, liveExample, marketMap, population, scorecard, url, usd } from '../lib/data';

const m = marketMap();
const sc = scorecard();
const example = liveExample();
const { totals: t, coverage: cov } = m;

const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Isobath liquidation intelligence — Hyperliquid BTC',
  description: 'Forced-selling exposure and defensibility, with stated coverage.',
  dateModified: m.generated_at,
  variableMeasured: [
    { '@type': 'PropertyValue', name: 'forced_notional_usd', value: t.forced_notional_usd },
    { '@type': 'PropertyValue', name: 'cannot_defend_pct', value: t.cannot_defend_pct },
    { '@type': 'PropertyValue', name: 'coverage', value: cov.observed_fraction },
  ],
};

---

<Base
  title="Check your liquidation risk on Hyperliquid — Isobath"
  description="Paste a Hyperliquid address and see how close it is to liquidation, and whether it has anything left to defend with. Free, no signup."
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <h1>Are you about to be liquidated?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Paste your Hyperliquid address. You will see how far price has to move before your position is
    closed for you — and the thing most tools leave out: whether you have anything left to defend
    it with.
  </p>

  <form class="checkform" action={url('check.html')} method="get" autocomplete="off">
    <input name="a" type="text" spellcheck="false" placeholder="0x…" aria-label="Hyperliquid address" />
    <button type="submit">Check</button>
  </form>
  <p class="hint">
    Free, no signup, nothing stored. The lookup happens in your browser, straight to Hyperliquid.
    {example && <a href={`${url('check.html')}?a=${example}`}>See a real one →</a>}
  </p>

  <AddressHelp />

  <h2>Why not just read a liquidation map?</h2>
  <p>
    Because the number every map shows you is the amount at risk, and the number that decides
    whether you survive is whether you can move your own liquidation price. That depends on free
    collateral — and the obvious way to compute it is wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    We checked the arithmetic against the exchange's own figure on 74 wallets. It agreed
    <strong>19%</strong> of the time and called <strong>one wallet in five</strong> able to defend
    itself when it was not. Always in the direction of looking safer.
    <a href={url('research/free-collateral-misclassification.html')}>The measurement →</a>
  </p>

  {/* NOT "right now". F-0012 measured that this figure is very nearly a constant -- median 100%
      across 433 published clusters, 71% at exactly 100%. Framing a constant as a live reading
      invites the reader to infer something from its level, and there is nothing to infer. The
      durable claim is more interesting anyway, and nobody else is able to make it. */}
  <h2>Almost nothing near liquidation can defend itself</h2>

  <p class="lede">
    Not today — <em>always</em>. Across every scan we have taken, the overwhelming majority of
    forced BTC exposure sits with wallets holding zero free collateral. They cannot move their own
    liquidation price without depositing from outside.
    <a href={url('research/vulnerability-does-not-vary.html')}>We measured how constant it is →</a>
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    In the latest scan: <strong>{usd(t.cannot_defend_usd)}</strong> of
    {usd(t.forced_notional_usd)} within ±10% of spot.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:1.6rem">
    <Prov
      tier="observed"
      detail={`${population(cov.tier, t.wallets_in_band)} · ${Math.round(cov.observed_fraction * 100)}% of open interest observed · ${age(m.map_age_seconds)} old`}
    />
    <br /><a href={url('markets/btc.html')}>The full BTC map →</a>
  </p>

  <Note title="What Isobath does not tell you.">
    It does not predict price. We tested whether reaching a liquidation cluster causes a further
    move and found it does not move price more than an ordinary volatile minute in the same hour.
    <a href={url('research/liquidations-dont-move-price.html')}>Read the test →</a>
  </Note>

  <h2>What we have published</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    {sc.findings.length} claims, {sc.counts.REFUTED ?? 0} of them refuted by us. Refuted findings are
    never removed.
  </p>
  <ul class="plain">
    {sc.findings.slice(0, 5).map((f) => (
      <li><span class={`tag ${f.status}`}>{f.status}</span>&nbsp; {f.title}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><a href={url('record.html')}>The full record →</a></p>
</Base>
